__d(function(e,a,t,i,o,r,n){o.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science",textbook:"Social and Political Life \u2014 Part I (Civics)",chapter_number:27,chapter_title:"Urban Administration",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the three types of Urban Local Bodies in India?",back:"The three types of Urban Local Bodies (ULBs) are: (1) Municipal Corporation (Mahanagara Palike) \u2014 for large cities with population typically over 5 lakh. (2) Municipal Council (City/Town Municipal Council) \u2014 for medium-sized towns. (3) Town Panchayat \u2014 for small towns. As a town grows, its ULB type is upgraded from Town Panchayat \u2192 Municipal Council \u2192 Municipal Corporation.",memory_hook:"Small-Medium-Large = Panchayat-Council-Corporation. Like shirt sizes \u2014 the bigger the city, the more powerful the local body."},{id:"fc02",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the full form of BBMP and what does it govern?",back:"BBMP stands for Bruhat Bengaluru Mahanagara Palike. It is the Municipal Corporation governing Bengaluru \u2014 India's IT capital and third-largest city. BBMP covers over 800 sq km, serves more than 1.2 crore residents, and is divided into 243 wards. It is one of India's largest and most complex urban local bodies.",memory_hook:"BBMP = Bengaluru's Big Municipal Power. 243 wards, 800 sq km, 1.2 crore people \u2014 India's IT city run by BBMP."},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a ward in urban administration? How is it used?",back:"A ward is a small electoral division of a city or town. Cities are divided into wards to ensure every neighbourhood has its own elected representative. Each ward elects one councillor. Bengaluru (BBMP) has 243 wards, each with approximately 30,000\u201350,000 residents. The ward is the basic unit of urban democratic representation.",memory_hook:"Ward = neighbourhood's voting unit. Like a constituency but for your street/locality. Your ward = your electoral home in the city."},{id:"fc04",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Who is a councillor and what is their role?",back:"A councillor is an elected representative of a ward in the urban local body. They are elected by all adult voters of the ward every 5 years. Roles: represent residents' concerns in municipal council meetings; take up neighbourhood issues (broken roads, garbage, drainage) with the municipal administration; chair ward committee meetings; and participate in budget decisions for the city.",memory_hook:"Councillor = your neighbourhood's voice in City Hall. The councillor is your most accessible elected official for local urban problems."},{id:"fc05",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the difference between the Mayor and the Municipal Commissioner?",back:"Mayor: ELECTED by councillors (or directly by citizens in some cities). Political head of the Municipal Corporation. Chairs council meetings, represents the city, provides political leadership. Municipal Commissioner: APPOINTED by the state government \u2014 a senior IAS officer. Executive/administrative head. Manages daily operations, implements council decisions, supervises all municipal departments and staff.",memory_hook:"Mayor = elected boss (political). Commissioner = appointed boss (administrative). City needs BOTH: a political leader (Mayor) and a professional administrator (Commissioner)."},{id:"fc06",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Which Constitutional Amendment gave constitutional status to Urban Local Bodies?",back:"The 74th Constitutional Amendment (1992) gave constitutional status to Urban Local Bodies. It mandated: elections every 5 years, reservation of seats for women (33%) and SC/ST communities, establishment of Ward Committees in cities with population over 3 lakh, and creation of State Finance Commissions to allocate funds to ULBs. It is the urban equivalent of the 73rd Amendment for rural Panchayati Raj.",memory_hook:"74 = 73 + 1. 73rd = rural Panchayati Raj. 74th = urban Municipal bodies. Both in 1992, both gave constitutional power to local self-government."},{id:"fc07",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"List five functions of a Municipal Corporation.",back:"(1) Maintain city roads, footpaths, bridges, flyovers. (2) Supply drinking water \u2014 operate treatment plants, maintain pipes. (3) Collect garbage and manage sanitation \u2014 public toilets, sewage treatment. (4) Maintain parks, playgrounds, and street lights. (5) Issue birth and death certificates; run primary health centres and municipal schools. Also: grant building permissions, regulate construction, manage markets.",memory_hook:"WISH+LP: Water, Infrastructure, Sanitation, Health + Lights, Permits. What does a city resident expect every day? The corporation provides it."},{id:"fc08",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is property tax and why is it important for Municipal Corporations?",back:"Property tax is an annual tax paid by owners of buildings and land within the city to the Municipal Corporation. It is calculated based on the value and size of the property. Property tax is the most important own-source revenue for corporations \u2014 it funds roads, garbage trucks, water pipes, park maintenance, and street light electricity. Without property tax, the corporation cannot fund basic city services.",memory_hook:"Property tax = city's salary. Just as employees pay to keep the office running, property owners pay taxes to keep the city running. PAY TAX \u2192 GET SERVICES. Simple contract between citizen and city."},{id:"fc09",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a Ward Committee? Why is it called the 'urban Gram Sabha'?",back:"A Ward Committee is a sub-body within each ward (in cities over 3 lakh population) that includes the elected councillor and representatives of residents' associations, NGOs, and civil society. It allows citizens to: discuss neighbourhood priorities, review ward expenditure, flag maintenance issues, and participate in planning. It is called the 'urban Gram Sabha' because \u2014 like the Gram Sabha in villages \u2014 it allows ordinary citizens to participate directly in local governance beyond just voting.",memory_hook:"Ward Committee = city's Gram Sabha. Rural \u2192 Gram Sabha. Urban \u2192 Ward Committee. Same democratic idea: let citizens directly participate, not just vote once in 5 years."},{id:"fc10",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why do cities need a separate local body instead of being managed by the state government directly?",back:"Cities have unique, dense, and complex needs that require specialised local management: (1) Scale \u2014 millions of people in a small area need daily services (garbage, water, roads). (2) Local knowledge \u2014 city residents know their neighbourhood's needs better than state officials in a distant capital. (3) Speed \u2014 pothole repairs and garbage collection need quick local decisions, not state-level bureaucracy. (4) Democratic participation \u2014 citizens should elect those who manage their immediate environment. A general state government cannot micro-manage every city's day-to-day affairs.",memory_hook:"State governs the whole state. City needs someone who only thinks about the city. Local problems need local government. The more specific the problem, the more local the solution needs to be."},{id:"fc11",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How can a citizen in Bengaluru use BBMP's systems to complain about a broken road?",back:"(1) BBMP App \u2014 'Bengaluru Bruhat Namma BBMP' app allows citizens to photograph and report road damage, waterlogging, or garbage issues. The complaint gets a ticket number. (2) BBMP Website \u2014 file complaints at bbmp.gov.in. (3) BBMP Helpline \u2014 call the BBMP helpline for urgent issues. (4) Ward Councillor \u2014 directly contact the elected ward councillor who can escalate the issue to the BBMP administration. (5) Ward Committee \u2014 raise it at the ward committee meeting.",memory_hook:"5 paths to report: App \u2192 Website \u2192 Helpline \u2192 Councillor \u2192 Ward Committee. Escalate from digital to personal to collective as needed."},{id:"fc12",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the main sources of income for a Municipal Corporation?",back:"(1) Property Tax \u2014 most important own revenue; annual tax on buildings/land. (2) Professional Tax \u2014 paid by salaried employees and professionals (doctors, lawyers). (3) Fees and Charges \u2014 building permission fees, water connection fees, parking fees, marriage registration fees. (4) State Government Grants \u2014 from State Finance Commission. (5) Central Government Grants \u2014 Smart Cities Mission, AMRUT scheme funds. Property tax is by far the largest own-source revenue; grants are the largest total source for most ULBs.",memory_hook:"PPFSC: Property tax, Professional tax, Fees, State grants, Central grants. Or: 'People Pay For Sustainable Cities' = Property-Professional-Fees-State-Centre."},{id:"fc13",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is property tax important for city governance, and what happens when citizens do not pay it?",back:"Property tax funds essential city services: road repair, garbage collection, park maintenance, water supply, street light electricity. When citizens do not pay: (1) The corporation has less money to maintain services \u2014 roads deteriorate, parks decay. (2) The corporation must borrow more or cut services. (3) Tax defaulters may face penalties, and ultimately the corporation can attach (seize) the property. The health of a city's services is directly linked to the tax payment discipline of its residents.",memory_hook:"No tax = no service. It is that simple. Every pothole you complain about is partly caused by the neighbour who did not pay property tax. City = everyone's shared responsibility."},{id:"fc14",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name four Karnataka cities that have Municipal Corporations (apart from Bengaluru).",back:"Karnataka has city corporations in: (1) Mysuru (Mysuru City Corporation \u2014 MCC), (2) Mangaluru (Mangaluru City Corporation), (3) Hubballi-Dharwad (Hubballi-Dharwad Municipal Corporation \u2014 HDMC), (4) Kalaburagi (Kalaburagi City Corporation). Also: Belagavi (Belagavi City Corporation), Tumakuru, and Davanagere have city corporations. Karnataka has one of the highest numbers of city corporations in South India.",memory_hook:"Karnataka's city corporation family: BBMP (Bengaluru) + Mysuru + Mangaluru + Hubballi-Dharwad + Kalaburagi + Belagavi + Tumakuru + Davanagere. Eight major corporations!"},{id:"fc15",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A building near you is being constructed without proper safety fencing and waste is being dumped on the road. Which body is responsible and how can residents act?",back:"The Municipal Corporation (BBMP in Bengaluru) is responsible for: (1) Granting building permissions \u2014 if construction began without permission, it is illegal. (2) Regulating construction sites \u2014 safety standards, waste management. (3) Maintaining roads \u2014 the road cannot be used as a dump. Residents can: file a complaint on the BBMP app or website; contact the ward councillor; the BBMP's enforcement wing (Bruhat Bengaluru Area Demolition Authority) can issue stop-work orders and penalties for illegal construction.",memory_hook:"Illegal construction = BBMP's problem to solve. Citizens report \u2192 BBMP inspects \u2192 penalties or stop-work. The ward councillor accelerates action."},{id:"fc16",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Compare the 73rd and 74th Constitutional Amendments.",back:"73rd Amendment (1992) \u2014 covers RURAL local bodies (Panchayati Raj): Gram Panchayat, Panchayat Samiti, Zila Parishad. 74th Amendment (1992) \u2014 covers URBAN local bodies (Municipal): Town Panchayat, Municipal Council, Municipal Corporation. Both passed in 1992. Both mandate elections every 5 years, reservation for women (33%) and SC/ST communities, and State Finance Commissions. Both aim to devolve power to local elected bodies \u2014 one for villages, one for cities.",memory_hook:"73 = Rural, 74 = Urban. Twins born in 1992. Same DNA (reservations, elections, devolution) \u2014 one for villages, one for cities."},{id:"fc17",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the Smart Cities Mission and how does it connect to urban administration in Karnataka?",back:"Smart Cities Mission is a central government scheme (launched 2015) that provides funding and technical support to selected cities to develop smart, sustainable urban infrastructure \u2014 better roads, public transport, digital governance, waste management, and citizen services. Karnataka's Smart Cities: Bengaluru, Mangaluru, Hubballi-Dharwad, Belagavi, Davanagere, and Shivamogga. The funds are managed by the Municipal Corporations in partnership with Smart City SPVs (Special Purpose Vehicles). It supplements property tax revenue for large-scale urban development.",memory_hook:"Smart Cities = central money for city upgrades. Karnataka has 6 Smart Cities \u2014 central government backs local bodies with extra funds for modern infrastructure."},{id:"fc18",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is Bengaluru's BBMP considered one of India's most complex urban local bodies to govern?",back:"BBMP's complexity comes from: (1) Scale \u2014 800+ sq km area, 1.2+ crore population (larger than many countries). (2) Rapid growth \u2014 Bengaluru grew from 50 sq km (1941) to 800+ sq km (2023) \u2014 extreme infrastructure demands. (3) Diversity \u2014 IT professionals, migrant workers, old city residents, slum dwellers \u2014 very different needs in one city. (4) Infrastructure \u2014 IT corridors, metro, lakes, old drainage systems \u2014 all must be maintained simultaneously. (5) Political complexity \u2014 243 councillors from competing parties must agree on city decisions.",memory_hook:"BBMP complexity = rapid growth + massive scale + diverse population + old infrastructure + new IT demands. The hardest exam question in Indian urban governance."},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does the Ward Committee give a citizen more power than just voting every 5 years?",back:"Voting is once every 5 years \u2014 indirect participation. Ward Committees allow: (1) Regular meetings where residents directly raise neighbourhood issues. (2) Review of ward expenditure \u2014 how the ward's share of BBMP funds is being spent. (3) Priority setting \u2014 which road to repair, where to add a footpath. (4) Immediate accountability \u2014 if the councillor is present and residents raise an issue publicly, they must respond. (5) Collective voice \u2014 50 residents raising the same issue at a ward committee is more powerful than one resident's complaint online.",memory_hook:"Ward Committee = democracy between elections. Elections give you a vote. Ward committees give you a voice \u2014 every month, not every 5 years."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A city is growing rapidly and its Town Municipal Council seems unable to handle the population and infrastructure needs. What should happen administratively?",back:"When a town's population and complexity grow beyond the capacity of its current Urban Local Body, the state government can upgrade its status: Town Panchayat \u2192 City Municipal Council \u2192 City Municipal Corporation. The upgrade brings: a larger elected council, a Municipal Commissioner (IAS officer), more powers and functions, higher borrowing capacity, and eligibility for larger central government grants (Smart Cities, AMRUT). Karnataka has upgraded several towns \u2014 e.g., Tumakuru and Davanagere were upgraded to City Corporations as their populations and infrastructure needs grew.",memory_hook:"Growing town = growing governance. The ULB upgrades as the city upgrades. Small \u2192 medium \u2192 large = Panchayat \u2192 Council \u2192 Corporation. Karnataka's Tumakuru = real example of this upgrade."}]}},2936,[]);